### AUC-optimal versus profit-optimal

Youden's J maximises true-positive rate minus false-positive rate, which is
optimal only if a missed fraud and a false decline carry equal weight per unit
rate. Here they do not. The profit-optimal cutoff is
{{ A.t_profit | num(4) }} against Youden-J's {{ A.t_youden | num(4) }}.
The Youden-J cutoff declines {{ A.decline_rate_ratio_youden_over_profit | num(1) }}
times as many transactions. The difference in test net benefit is
{{ C.profit_single_minus_youden_single | ci_usd(2) }} per
{{ r.meta.per_n_transactions | int }} transactions, or
{{ A.money_left_on_table_total_test | usd }} across the test period.

On validation, where both were chosen: Youden-J net benefit
{{ A.validation.youden_single.net_benefit_per_1000 | usd(2) }} and profit-optimal
{{ A.validation.profit_single.net_benefit_per_1000 | usd(2) }} per
{{ r.meta.per_n_transactions | int }}. The test oracle threshold
({{ A.test_oracle_threshold | num(4) }}, chosen with test labels, not achievable)
would have given {{ A.test_oracle_net_benefit_per_1000 | usd(2) }}.

